name: research-iteration
description: Iterate a research topic version by version, from an idea to a paper or writeup that can win or be accepted. Covers framing beyond "a competition report", finding or mining data that makes the claim general, prior-art and adversarial novelty passes, a simulated panel of elite reviewers from three vendors plus a blind design, lead verification of the key numbers, re-estimating the odds after each reframing, and an ambitious-but-realistic plan for the user to decide. Use when choosing or reframing a paper, Working Notes or writeup topic (選題、換題目、疊代題目); when a friend's idea or a conversation should become a paper; when a draft exists and the next version must be stronger; or when asked for the chance of winning or acceptance. Every step follows `research-standard`.
---

# Research iteration

Each pass through the loop produces one version of the topic (v1, v2, …). Keep it in the project under `paper/topic-iteration-<date>/`:
- `lessons.md`, `brief.md`, `prompts/`, `round-<n>/`, `synthesis-round-<n>.md`;
- the `.research/` ledger from `research init`.

Every reading step follows `research-standard`: full text, one level down, verified quotes, mismatches into the finding bank.

## Ask these questions yourself

These are the questions the user asked by hand across three paper sessions on 2026-11-02 (AIMO interpretability, IEEE EMBS BHI, a SAFE/ICSC venue scan). Each one forced a better version. Ask them before the user has to.

| Question | What it forces |
| --- | --- |
| Does this read like a competition or website report? | Lead with a question that needs a study design to answer. The competition appears once, as the data source. |
| Can more or better data make the claim general? Should we mine a dataset? | External or held-out datasets, blind cross-dataset validation, a resource others can reuse. |
| Has someone already done this? Thousands of entrants: would they not do the same? | Widened search plus an adversarial pass. The claim shrinks to what is actually new. |
| What would top reviewers from other fields say? | A panel (below). The AIMO panel withdrew a claim its own data refuted and found a sharper question. |
| Can you prove the conclusion is not a bug? Are there enough seeds and samples? | Independent recomputation. More independent units, not just seeds. |
| Does it only hold for one vendor or product? | Make the subject a class of signals or methods. |
| If you were ambitious but realistic, what would you do? | Ship the safe version; the next one answers "when, for whom, why". |
| Can our writing beat the other entries? Have you read the others' reports? | Read every public entry. Name what we found that they did not. |
| Is compute the reason we stopped? | Price the cheap path's risk and ask for compute instead of dropping a promising idea. |

## The loop

**0. Venue.** Read the rules, criteria and award structure in full; they change the target. Example: the Gemma 4 paper track allows two writeups per team and has a separate Best New Resource prize. Check the leaderboard, forum and other entrants' public work.

**1. Brief.** Write `brief.md`: the contest or venue, what we have (numbers with tags), the closest prior work (read in full), and 3–6 candidate directions with costs and risks. Write it for a smart outsider.

**2. Prior art.** Widen the search: a synonym grid, every source type, recent days. Then run an adversarial pass with `templates/adversarial_novelty.md`, on a different vendor from the main search. Fetch, read and verify every hit through the ledger before using it.

**3. Panel and blind design.**
- **Data rules first.** Before any outside model (codex, Gemini, another account) sees competition data, gated material or unpublished results, check the competition's data rules and the project's decisions file, and ask the user when unclear (competition-campaign, principle 7). This skill does not relax any project's data rules; when data may not leave, give that reviewer only the aggregated brief.
- **How to dispatch.** Run reviewers through the codex, `agy` and iphotutor dispatch scripts, on cores the coordinator lends. The Workflow tool is used only when the user asks for a workflow.
- **Reviewers:**
  - six simulated reviewers from `templates/panel_reviewer.md`;
  - fields spread wide, e.g. machine learning, software engineering, probability and statistics, analysis or combinatorics, causal inference or econometrics, experimental physics or measurement;
  - personas of expertise only; never impersonate or quote real people;
  - spread across three vendors where possible: codex (gpt-6.1-sol, effort ≤ high), Gemini through `agy`, and Claude.
- **Blind design:** one design from the official task alone (competition-campaign, Variant D).
- **Model quirks:**
  - Gemini models differ by account. Run `HOME=~/.gemini-accounts/<acct> agy models` first; on 11-02 account a2 had no argon model (the run exited 1), and `gemini-3.8-flash-high` worked.
  - Gemini runs in `--mode plan` without tools, so inline the material it may see (after the data-rules check above).
  - Gemini's win probabilities ran high. On Gemma (11-02), Gemini reviewers gave 25–30% Overall for a direction that codex and Claude put at about 12%. Treat every reviewer's probabilities as uncalibrated guesses; compare rankings, not raw percentages.

**4. Lead verification.**
- Recompute every number the panel's recommendation rests on, from the raw files.
- Read in full every new source the reviewers cite.
- Run the cheapest decisive test on existing data when it costs minutes, before reporting. Example: on Gemma, the observational "compaction cliff" was recomputed on our own runs before recommending the reframing.

**5. Synthesis and decision.** Write `synthesis_round<N>.md`:
- where reviewers agree and disagree, by vendor;
- the lead's checks;
- the recommended portfolio, with odds marked as guesses;
- the free work and the paid work, each with a cost line.

Then give the user one decision. Paid compute, uploads and outward messages each need the user's explicit consent.

**6. Reframe → repeat.** Every reframing reruns step 2 on the new claim and re-estimates the odds, with the reasons.

**7. Before submission.**
- the `research-standard` gate (`research check <doc> [--bib refs.bib] --strict`);
- an independent full pre-review (`paper-pre-review`);
- `paper-readability-pass`;
- figures per the paper-figures rule.

## Gotchas

- **Seeds are not tasks.** Extra replicates do not shrink an interval driven by few independent tasks or subjects. Power arithmetic counts independent units.
- **A panel's favourite claim can be refuted by our own data.** Check it before writing the title (step 4).
- **Pre-registered kill tests are commitments** (`research-standard` §5).
- **Report the unknowns.** Record unanswered organiser questions (e.g. "can two writeups be submitted at once?") and failed agents (wrong model name, sandbox without network) in the synthesis. Never drop them silently.
