PAGES 22
PAGE 1 Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2026, pages 40260–40281
October 24-29, 2026 ©2026 Association for Computational Linguistics
Does Item Response Theory Generalize Across Languages?
Evidence from Cross-Lingual LLM Benchmarks
Ruchira Dhar† Qiwei Peng† Anders Søgaard†
†Department of Computer Science, University of Copenhagen
Abstract
The application of Item Response Theory (IRT)
for data selection in benchmarks has mostly
been limited to monolingual settings. In this
work, we ask: how does IRT generalize when
the same benchmark is translated across lan-
guages? We conduct experiments on Multilin-
gual GSM and Global MMLU, covering over
100 language models and 11 languages. Fitting
1PL, 2PL, and 3PL IRT models per language,
we assess cross-lingual consistency of parame-
ters and whether IRT-filtered subsets preserve
model rankings across languages: item diffi-
culty emerges as the most stable parameter fol-
lowed by guessing and then discrimination. A
differential item analysis also shows that lack of
stability can be tracked to specific items shift-
ing under translation across languages. Our
findings suggest IRT-based selection in multi-
lingual settings must be both parameter-aware
and item-aware, validating model choice and
screening individual items before assuming
cross-lingual generalization.
1 Introduction
Item Response Theory (IRT), a psychometric
framework that characterizes each item in a test
with parameters estimated from response patterns
across many subjects, has its origins in clinical
psychology (Harvey and Hammer, 1999) and edu-
cational testing (Yen and Fitzpatrick, 2006; Lord,
2012). In empirical NLP research, IRT has been
used as a tool for selecting data points by filtering
test-sets for model evaluation since not all evalua-
tions examples turn out to be equally informative
(Lalor et al., 2016; Natesan et al., 2016; Lalor et al.,
2019; Rodriguez et al., 2022). More recently, it
has also been shown that fitting IRT models to
LLM responses, we can select data points while
preserving ranking validity of the full benchmark
(Hofmann et al., 2025; Zhou et al., 2026). How-
ever, the vast majority of this work has been con-
ducted in monolingual settings, primarily English
(see Appendix A for related work). Today a key
focus of NLP is cross-lingual evaluation (Hu et al.,
2020; Doddapaneni et al., 2025; Chollampatt et al.,
2025) where translation is a popular strategy for
constructing multilingual benchmarks (Shi et al.,
2023; Singh et al., 2025). This raises a natural but
largely untested concern: how does IRT generalize
cross-lingually when the same benchmark is trans-
lated across languages? To our knowledge, this is
the first systematic study to attempt to address this.
We evaluate 102 LMs on MGSM (Shi et al.,
2023) (11 languages giving 11 slices) and 103 LMs
on Global MMLU (Singh et al., 2025) (7 languages,
6 domains giving 42 slices), yielding over 568,000
item-response pairs across 53 benchmark slices.
We fit three IRT variants with incrementally more
parameters and increasing complexity: 1PL ( dif-
ficulty only), 2PL (adds discrimination), and 3PL
(adds guessing), independently to each benchmark
slice, yielding 159 fitted models.We ask three ques-
tions: 1) are IRT parameters consistent across trans-
lated versions of a benchmark; 2) do IRT-filtered
subsets preserve model rankings across languages?;
and 3) can cross-lingual instability in IRT parame-
ters be traced to specific items rather than treated
as a language-wide effect? Our results indicate that
item difficulty shows highest concordance across
languages for all IRT models, and guessing per-
forms better than discrimination. This parameter
hierarchy directly determines subset validity: 1PL
is the most reliable cross-lingual variant. A differ-
ential item analysis also confirms this instability
is item-level and not language-wide effects. We
further observe that for MMLU, scientific domains
are consistently more concordant across languages
as compared to non-science domains.
Taken together, our results indicate that IRT does
not generalise uniformly across languages: which
parameters and subsets transfer depends on the
IRT model, the individual item, and the domain of
evaluation.
40260
PAGE 2 2 Methodology
2.1 LLM Evaluations
We use two multilingual translated benchmarks:
Multilingual GSM (MGSM) (Shi et al., 2023)
across 11 languages ( bn, de, en, es, fr, ja, ru,
sw, te, th, zh), yielding 250 items per language
and 2,750 items per model; Global MMLU (Singh
et al., 2025) across 7 languages ( de, en, es, fr,
ja, sw, zh) and 6 subject domains ( business, hu-
manities, medical, other, social sciences, stem),
totaling 400 items per language and 2,800 items
per model under the standard 7-language configu-
ration. For models, we use the HuggingFace Open
LLM Leaderboard to select the top instruction-
tuned models under 20 billion parameters that are
compatible with the lm-eval-harness framework
(Gao et al., 2024), yielding 102 and 103 compatible
models for MGSM and Global MMLU respectively.
In total, our corpus comprises 568,900 evaluated
item–response pairs, each consisting of the model’s
generated response to a benchmark question and a
binary correctness label (implementation details in
Appendix B). These item–response pairs are used
for IRT model training and for all downstream anal-
ysis1.
2.2 IRT Model Training
The response data, consisting of binary correctness
indicators Xij ∈ {0, 1} representing whether a spe-
cific language model i answered a benchmark item
j correctly, are organised into one response matrix
per language (for MGSM) and one per language–
domain combination (for Global MMLU), yielding
53 matrices in total (11 MGSM × 1 + 7 MMLU
languages × 6 domains). We fit three IRT model
variants to each matrix independently (implemen-
tation details in Appendix B). For 1PL, we use
Stochastic Variational Inference (SVI) as imple-
mented in py-irt (Natesan et al., 2016) on top of
Pyro (Bingham et al., 2019). For 2PL and 3PL, we
use MAP-based implementation in PyTorch 2. We
fit the following IRT model variants:
• 1PL (Rasch model). The response probability
is P (Xij = 1) = σ(θi − bj), where θi is the
1The code is publicly available at this GitHub Repo.
2We retain py-irt’s SVI for 1PL, where SVI and MAP
estimates are highly concordant, confirming inference method
does not affect results. For 2PL and 3PL, SVI’s prior dom-
inates gradient updates at LLM-scale and MAP avoids this.
See Appendix C the full comparison and justification.
ability of model i and bj is the difficulty of
item j.
• 2PL. The response probability is P (Xij =
1) = σ(aj(θi − bj)), adding a per-item dis-
crimination parameter aj > 0 that scales how
sharply the item distinguishes between high-
and low-ability models.
• 3PL. The response probability is P (Xij =
1) = cj + (1−cj) σ(aj(θi − bj)), where cj ∈
[0, 1] is a lower asymptote (guessing parame-
ter) that models the probability of a correct re-
sponse by a minimally-capable model, captur-
ing chance-level or knowledge-independent
success on an item.
For 1PL, we use py-irt’s SVI with dropout
(p = 0.5), vague priors, and learning rate η = 0.1,
training for 2,000 epochs (selected at the point of
ELBO stabilisation) and retaining parameters at
the minimum-ELBO epoch. For 2PL and 3PL, we
use MAP with Adam ( η = 0 .05) for 4,000 and
6,000 epochs respectively (selected at the point of
MAP objective stabilisation, with longer schedules
reflecting increased model complexity), retaining
the best-objective state across training.
2.3 IRT Analysis
We conduct three sets of experiments to assess
how IRT parameters generalize across language
versions of these benchmarks .
Cross-lingual Consistency Analysis (§3.1) ex-
amines whether item parameters are stable across
languages using four measures 3: (i) Kendall’s
W ∈ [0, 1], the coefficient of concordance across
all |L| languages simultaneously (W = 1: identi-
cal item rankings; W ≈ 0: chance); (ii) Spearman
Correlations ρ ∈ [−1, 1], the monotonic rank cor-
relation between each language pair, displayed as a
|L| × |L|heatmap; (iii) Per-Item Rank Deviation,
the standard deviation of each item’s difficulty rank
across languages, where high values flag unstable
items; (iv) Language Clustering (average linkage
on Spearman distance 1 − ρ) grouping languages
by similarity of their IRT profiles, with alignment
to accuracy-based clusters quantified via Mantel
test.
3All IRT variants achieve monotonically improving within-
language fit across majority of slices (3PL > 2PL > 1PL;
Appendix I), suggesting that cross-lingual parameter differ-
ences in following analyses are not merely artifacts of poor
within-language fit.
