Business, Hum: Humanities, Med: Medical, SS: Social Sciences, Oth: Others).
Subset Analysis (§3.2) evaluates IRT filtering
along two dimensions. (i) Cross-Model IIF Con-
cordance: items are ranked by their Item Infor-
mation Function (IIF) at θ = 0 under each IRT
model variant, and pairwise Jaccard similarity of
the top-50% subsets is measured across benchmark
slices to assess whether model choice affects item
selection; (ii) Subset Ranking Fidelity: Spearman
ρ between model accuracy on the IRT-selected sub-
set and on the full benchmark, evaluated within-
language and cross-lingually under in-sample and
held-out (80/20) model splits.
DIF Analysis (§3.3) asks whether cross-lingual
instability in IRT parameters is a language-wide
effect or is concentrated in specific items. Thus,
for each item we ask: once we account for the
fact that some languages are simply harder overall,
does this specific item still get noticeably harder or
easier when translated? An item where the answer
is yes is exhibiting differential item functioning
(DIF) and language-specific bias in how the item
behaves, independent of overall difficulty (Holland
and Wainer, 2012; Allalouf et al., 1999). To test
this directly, we conduct an anchor-item DIF anal-
ysis relative to English. For each language, the
bottom quartile of items by cross-lingual rank stan-
dard deviation serves as an anchor set to equate
the 1PL difficulty scale between English and each
