DIF Analysis. DIF is widespread and highly
item-specific: on MGSM an average of 48.3% of
items and on MMLU 31.8% of items show signif-
icant difficulty shifts relative to English and DIF
rates change (highest for Swahili and Telugu on
MGSM, Swahili on MMLU), indicating that the
same item-level translation pressures accumulate
more heavily for languages least related to the
source. We restrict this test to difficulty: discrim-
ination is already the least cross-lingually stable
parameter in §3.1, so no discrimination-invariant
anchor set can be identified to equate its scale, and
guessing’s lower-asymptote estimates require sub-
stantially larger per-item response counts than our
model pool provides to support individual-item
significance testing. Since difficulty is the most
cross-lingually stable of the three parameters and
still shows this much item-level heterogeneity once
tested directly, it is plausible that discrimination
and guessing, already less stable in aggregate, con-
ceal comparable or greater item-level heterogene-
ity that a suitably powered DIF test would need
to confirm. The practical implication is that this
instability is item-specific rather than an inherent
property of a language, and is in principle address-
able through targeted re-translation or removal with
item-level auditing.
More concretely, for using IRT-based data selec-
tion in benchmark filtering on multilingual trans-
lated benchmarks: (i) we must not assume that
a subset which works well within one language
will generalise to others, i.e., cross-lingual validity
should be verified explicitly; (ii) we must account
for domain composition, as STEM domains are re-
liably more cross-lingually stable than non-STEM,
and a single overall fidelity check will mask impor-
tant variation within mixed-domain benchmarks;
(iii) a subset can look valid by an aggregate con-
cordance check while still containing individual
items that behave inconsistently, so item-level DIF
screening should complement aggregate validity
checks before reusing a subset across languages.
Simpler models that rely only on the most stable
parameters will produce more transferable subsets.
5 Conclusion
We set out to test a basic but largely unexamined as-
sumption in multilingual LLM evaluation: whether
IRT, an established tool for benchmark filtering,
behaves consistently when the same benchmark is
translated across languages. The answer is that it
does, but only for some parameters, and even for
the parameter that transfers best, only for some
items rather than all of them. IRT parameters form
a cross-lingual stability hierarchy: difficulty trans-
fers reliably, discrimination does not, and guessing
sits in between. Even difficulty, the most stable pa-
rameter, still shows significant translation-induced
shifts in roughly a third to half of items once each
language’s overall difficulty is accounted for, show-
ing that cross-lingual instability is not simply a
property of a language but of specific items within
it.
As translation-based multilingual benchmarks
become common, the psychometric properties of
translated items cannot be assumed to carry over
across languages. Our findings show that the choice
of IRT model, the stability of its parameters, and
the presence of item-level differences determine
whether a compressed benchmark remains valid
across the languages it is meant to evaluate. Cross-
lingual validity should be demonstrated, not as-
sumed, and we hope this work, combining aggre-
gate concordance checks with item-level auditing,
provides a concrete empirical basis for that stan-
dard.
Limitations
We discuss some limitations of our work here.First,
our model set is restricted to instruction-tuned mod-
els with fewer than 20 billion parameters, due to
compute constraints. This means our IRT param-
eter estimates and subset fidelity results may not
generalize to larger frontier models, whose ability
profiles could alter item difficulty and discrimina-
tion rankings in ways not captured here.
Second, our experiments are conducted on two
benchmarks, MGSM and Global MMLU, which,
while covering a broad range of languages and
domains, are both derived from English source
items via translation. Extending this analysis to
benchmarks constructed natively in non-English
languages, or to tasks beyond multiple-choice and
arithmetic reasoning, remains an important direc-
tion for future work. Additionally, the Global
MMLU subset used here includes four European
40268
PAGE 10 languages (German, English, Spanish, French) out
of seven, which may inflate cross-lingual concor-
dance estimates relative to a more typologically
diverse sample.
Ethical Considerations
We do not anticipate any risks in our work. Any
data used does not contain any personal details or
offensive content. In this study, our use of all arti-
facts is consistent with the license and intended use
of all datasets and models. The benchmarks used,
Multilingual GSM (Shi et al., 2023) and Global
MMLU(Singh et al., 2025) are available on Hug-
gingFace for free use. All large language models
used are also open from HuggingFace. The py-irt
library used for 1PL model fitting is open source
and freely available under MIT License. Our MAP-
based implementation for 2PL and 3PL is imple-
mented in PyTorch and will be released alongside
the codebase upon publication.
