C SVI vs. MAP Inference Comparison
To verify that our choice of inference method
does not confound cross-model comparisons, we
fit a MAP-based 1PL to all 53 benchmark slices
alongside MAP-based 2PL and 3PL, and compare
difficulty, discrimination, and guessing estimates
against their SVI counterparts (Figure 12).
For 1PL, SVI and MAP difficulty estimates are
highly concordant (mean ρ = 0.928, min = 0.749,
max = 0.996), confirming that using py-irt’s SVI
for 1PL does not affect our results.
For 2PL and 3PL, the comparison directly illus-
trates why MAP is necessary. Discrimination esti-
mates are near-uncorrelated across the two methods
(2PL: mean ρ = 0.130; 3PL: mean ρ = −0.021):
SVI drives discrimination toward zero (2PL) or
negative values (3PL), while MAP with softplus-
constrained discrimination recovers psychometri-
cally meaningful positive values. Under 3PL the
distortion is severe enough to corrupt even diffi-
culty estimates (mean ρ = −0.059). The guess-
ing parameter is relatively robust across inference
methods (mean ρ = 0.717), consistent with its role
as a lower-asymptote constraint less sensitive to
discrimination interactions.
D Split-Half Analysis
To verify that discrimination and guessing parame-
ters are reliably estimated under MAP rather than
under-identified given our model pool size, we con-
duct a within-language split-half reliability check.
For each of the 11 MGSM languages, we randomly
partition the full N =102 model pool into two dis-
joint halves (N =51 each) and independently refit
2PL/3PL via MAP on each half, repeating this for
3 random splits per language (33 fits per model in
total).
Agreement between the two halves is strong:
mean Spearman ρ = 0 .614 for 2PL discrimina-
tion ([0.424, 0.754]), ρ = 0.621 for 3PL discrim-
ination ( [0.382, 0.762]), and ρ = 0 .708 for 3PL
guessing ( [0.517, 0.872]), despite each half con-
taining roughly half as many models as the full
sample used in the paper. Each half also correlates
strongly with the full-sample (N =102) estimates
reported in the main text: ρ ∈ [0.776, 0.931] (mean
0.866) for 2PL discrimination, ρ ∈ [0.748, 0.917]
(mean 0.860) for 3PL discrimination, and ρ ∈
[0.708, 0.973] (mean 0.874) for 3PL guessing.
Guessing spread at N =51 is nearly identical to
the full sample (mean σ = 0.049 vs. 0.051); dis-
crimination spread is somewhat reduced at N =51
(mean σ = 0 .445 vs. 0.578 for 2PL, 0.418 vs.
0.549 for 3PL), consistent with expected shrink-
age toward the MAP prior under a smaller model
pool, though item rankings by discrimination are
preserved, as shown by the correlations above. To-
gether, these results indicate that discrimination
and guessing are reliably recoverable within a lan-
guage at half the model pool size, and that their low
cross-lingual concordance (Appendix F) reflects
cross-lingual instability rather than under-identified
noise.
E Correlated Raters Analysis
Kendall’s W aggregates agreement across raters
(here, languages); a tight cluster of correlated raters
could inflate the statistic even if the remaining
raters disagree substantially, analogous to non-
independent raters inflating inter-rater reliability.
section 3.1 shows that European languages form
exactly such a tight cluster under item difficulty. To
test whether this cluster is responsible for the con-
cordance values reported in §3.1, we recompute
W after removing all Indo-European languages:
German, English, Spanish, French, Bengali, and
Russian for MGSM (5 languages remain: Japanese,
