Swahili, Telugu, Thai, Chinese), and German, En-
glish, Spanish, and French for MMLU (3 languages
remain: Japanese, Swahili, Chinese), the latter av-
eraged over its six domains (results in Table 5).
Removing the Indo-European cluster increases
W in every case: MGSM rises from 0.544 →
0.560 (1PL), 0.526 → 0.553 (2PL), and 0.519 →
0.547 (3PL); MMLU rises from 0.592 → 0.606
(1PL), 0.540 → 0.574 (2PL), and 0.582 → 0.593
(3PL). If the Indo-European cluster were propping
up the reported concordance, its removal should
have lowered W ; instead, the more typologically
distinct remaining languages reproduce the same
signal on their own. The MMLU restricted set
has only three languages and its estimates there-
fore carry more sampling variance than the seven-
language originals, but the same pattern holds on
MGSM’s larger five-language restricted set. These
results indicate that the reported concordance val-
ues are not an artifact of rater redundancy.
F Spearman Rank Correlation Heatmaps
In main text, Figure 2 shows pairwise Spearman
rank correlations for item difficulty while Figure 3
provides the corresponding heatmaps for discrimi-
nation (a, available from 2PL onward) and guess-
ing (c, 3PL only). Here, we provide a more detailed
discussion of findings:
Discrimination (a). Discrimination parameters
show low cross-lingual agreement under both 2PL
and 3PL. For MGSM 2PL, the mean pairwise
ρ = 0 .103 (range [−0.068 (English–Japanese)
to 0.275 (German–Russian)]): while the esti-
mates are positive and psychometrically mean-
ingful within each language, they do not agree
strongly across languages, indicating that how
sharply an item discriminates between models is
a language-specific property of item formulation.
Under MGSM 3PL the mean pairwise ρ = 0.098
(range [−0.085, 0.241]), consistent with the addi-
tional guessing parameter absorbing some residual
shared signal and leaving discrimination with lit-
tle cross-lingual structure. MMLU 2PL and 3PL
show a similarly low average discrimination agree-
ment (¯ρ ≈ 0.10 for both), indicating that even with
larger per-domain item pools, discrimination does
not generalize cross-lingually. This provides clear
evidence that discrimination captures language-
idiosyncratic surface features of item formulation
rather than a shared psychometric property.
Guessing ( c). The guessing parameter tells a
markedly different story. For MGSM 3PL,
¯ρ = 0 .349 (range [0.186, 0.537]), with Bengali–
German achieving the strongest agreement ( ρ =
0.537), a plausible finding if both language ver-
sions impose similar chance-level accessibility on
the same underlying items. English–Telugu is the
weakest pair (ρ = 0.186); all pairs remain positive.
For MMLU 3PL, guessing agreement is compa-
rably stable: ¯ρ = 0 .359 (range [0.138, 0.538]),
with every language pair yielding a positive corre-
lation and Japanese–Chinese the most consistent
(ρ = 0 .538). The consistently positive guessing
correlations, in contrast to near-zero or negative
discrimination correlations under the same model,
suggest that lower-bound response behavior is a
genuine language-invariant property: some items
simply impose a floor on the probability of a correct
response regardless of the language of presentation,
while discrimination is driven by language-specific
surface features of the item formulation.
Science vs Non-Science Domain Figure 13
shows the 2 ×3 grid of averaged Spearman dif-
ficulty heatmaps for science vs non-science do-
mains across the three IRT model variants. Un-
der 1PL, science heatmaps show a predominantly
warm palette (Kendall’s W = 0.647, ¯ρ = 0.588),
while non-science is already lower ( ¯ρ = 0 .492,
W = 0 .564). Under MAP-fitted 2PL and 3PL,
the science advantage is maintained: science do-
mains achieve ¯ρ = 0.559, W = 0.620 (2PL) and
¯ρ = 0.581, W = 0.640 (3PL), while non-science
remains somewhat lower at ¯ρ = 0.420, W = 0.501
(2PL) and ¯ρ = 0.479, W = 0.553 (3PL). Both do-
main types maintain moderate cross-lingual concor-
dance across all model variants, with science items
consistently showing stronger agreement. This re-
flects that science items test well-defined reason-
ing steps or unambiguous facts whose difficulty is
