J IIF Theta Sensitivity
The main analysis evaluates IIF at θ = 0 (median
ability). To assess sensitivity to this choice, we
recompute item selections at θ ∈ {−1, 0, +1} and
measure the Jaccard similarity of the resulting top-
50% subsets across theta values within each model,
and cross-model Jaccard at each theta.
Within-model sensitivity. Under MAP, 2PL
item selection shows meaningful theta sensitivity
on both benchmarks: MGSM 2PL meanJ = 0.639
between θ = −1 and θ = 0; J = 0.429 between
θ = 0 and θ = +1; J = 0.232 between θ = −1
40275
PAGE 17 and θ = +1. This reflects that properly-identified
discrimination parameters vary across items, so IIF
rankings depend on theta. 3PL shows similar sen-
sitivity (MGSM: J = 0.617, 0.400, 0.208 for the
same pairs). 1PL shows moderate theta sensitivity
on MGSM (mean J = 0.727 between θ = −1 and
θ = 0; J = 0 .835 between θ = 0 and θ = +1)
but near-invariance on MMLU (mean J ≥ 0.929).
The greater sensitivity on MGSM reflects the wider
difficulty range of arithmetic items relative to the
narrower MMLU domain slices.
Cross-model concordance across theta. Ta-
ble 10 shows median Jaccard for each model pair at
each θ. The qualitative ordering is preserved across
all theta values: 1PL–2PL and 1PL–3PL agreement
both remain above the random baseline (J ≈ 0.33)
at every θ, and 2PL–3PL agreement is consistently
high (J ≥ 0.87). The conclusion that 2PL and 3PL
select overlapping but not identical subsets relative
to 1PL therefore holds across all evaluated ability
levels.
K Compression Ratio Sensitivity
The main analysis uses a top-50% IIF cutoff. To
assess sensitivity to this choice, Table 11 reports
in-sample mean Spearman ρ (subset vs. full bench-
mark) at top-30%, top-50%, and top-70% for each
IRT variant and benchmark.
The qualitative ordering is preserved across all
ratios. 1PL is reliable at 50% and above on both
benchmarks; at 30%, fidelity dips slightly (MMLU
mean ρ = 0.931). 2PL is competitive on MGSM
at all compression levels (mean ρ ≥ 0.939), but
shows substantially lower fidelity on MMLU at
all thresholds (mean ρ = 0 .771–0.875), remain-
ing below 1PL. 3PL follows a similar pattern, with
MMLU fidelity lower than 2PL and 1PL at all ra-
tios (mean ρ = 0.674–0.721). The 50% default is
the point where 1PL clears the random baseline re-
liably across all slices while achieving meaningful
compression.
L Subset Ranking Fidelity
Here, we provide: per-slice fidelity across all
benchmark variants (Figure 16), and per-model
accuracy-prediction scatter for in-sample (Fig-
ure 17) and held-out (Figure 18) evaluations.
Per-slice Fidelity (Figure 16). Each dot is one
benchmark slice — 11 MGSM language slices
and 42 MMLU language ×domain slices. For
MGSM, 1PL is uniformly strong across all 11 lan-
guages (median ρ = 0 .982, IQR entirely above
ρ = 0.95), with no language slice falling below the
random baseline. 2PL is similarly strong (median
ρ = 0.971, IQR 0.957–0.976), with the minimum
at ρ = 0 .915, well above the random baseline;
no slice fails badly. 3PL is comparable (median
ρ = 0 .973, IQR 0.955–0.976, min 0.911). For
MMLU, the contrast between 1PL and the multi-
parameter models sharpens considerably. 1PL
achieves median ρ = 1.000 — the majority of the
42 slices reach perfect rank correlation — with all
slices well above the random baseline (ρ ≈ 0.70).
2PL falls to median ρ = 0.878 (IQR 0.740–0.970,
min 0.354): most MMLU slices retain useful fi-
delity, but the lower tail extends below the random
baseline on a minority of slices. 3PL is further re-
duced (median ρ = 0.764, IQR 0.594–0.892, min
0.290), with more slices falling below the reference
line than under 2PL.
In-sample accuracy prediction (Figure 17).
Each point is one (model, benchmark-slice) pair
(n = 1,122 for MGSM; n = 4,320 for MMLU).
The dashed y = x line marks perfect accuracy
prediction; deviations from it indicate systematic
bias in how the subset estimates a model’s full-
benchmark score. Under 1PL, MGSM points clus-
ter tightly near the diagonal (MAE = 0.086, bias
= +0.026): the subset very slightly overestimates
full accuracy, consistent with IIF at θ = 0 select-
ing items near median difficulty, which form a
marginally easier sub-pool than the benchmark as
a whole. Under 1PL, MMLU shows the opposite
bias (MAE = 0 .098, bias = −0.065): the IIF-
selected items are slightly harder than the MMLU
average because IIF peaks for items with diffi-
culty b ≈ 0, which are harder than the typical
MMLU item (most items are easier than chance
for these models). Consequently, subset accuracy
understates full-benchmark accuracy by about six
percentage points on average. Under 2PL and
3PL, MGSM accuracy prediction is close to 1PL
(MGSM 2PL MAE = 0.103, bias = +0.073; 3PL
MAE = 0.101, bias = +0.079). On MMLU, 2PL
IIF introduces a moderate positive bias ( +0.080,
MAE = 0 .117): the discrimination-weighted se-
lection still over-selects items where models score
high, inflating subset accuracy estimates relative to
the full benchmark by about 8 percentage points.
Held-out accuracy prediction (Figure 18). The
held-out evaluation re-fits IRT on 80% of models
